  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Independent Institute research fellow Jonathan Hofer argues that the growing density of ALPR networks may transform them into a de facto GPS tracking system, potentially triggering 4th Amendment search protections under the Mosaic Theory derived from U.S. v. Jones and Carpenter v. U.S. He reviews relevant case law (including Schmidt v. Norfolk, U.S. v. Martin, Rinaldi v. Sylvester, Commonwealth v. McCarthy) and proposes mandatory short retention periods as a partial remedy.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "as the density of these cameras in a given area increases, the individual snapshots effectively become a continuous stream, a kind of baton-passing from one camera to another to reveal a vehicle's travel patterns.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "It is reasonable to believe that a network of cameras of sufficient density would not pass constitutional muster unless offset by countervailing limits on their record-keeping.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "By default, some vendors purge data after 30 or 60 days. Already this appears overly long, but as the readers become more common, it's likely that public policy should require shorter retention periods.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    }
  ],
